{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791283195,"nonce":"wNSnPzEnCv00bxpLg33kkDTDwvo=","title":"Keep native Int64 identity and value across Dynamic","message":"Issue4014 fails Int64.isInt64 after assigning Int64.make(1, 1) to Dynamic. The compiler boxes the native i64 with TYPE_INT, while isInt64 expects the private __Int64 class token. That token hashes haxe.__Int64 instead of its typedef target haxe.___Int64.\n\nUse the backing class ID for both private type tokens and native Int64 boxes. A box exposes the standard class header and signed high/low slots, and unboxing reconstructs the full i64. Dynamic string conversion uses the native value rather than calling the class method with an object address as the value.\n\nAn independent regression exercises Dynamic locals, function parameters and returns, Array\u003cDynamic\u003e storage, class tokens, Dynamic high/low fields, string conversion, and the signed i64 limits. It caught a second defect: the Int64 unbox path skipped Dynamic parameters unless their registers were already marked as boxes, despite the earlier primitive-register check already handling raw native values.\n\nRelated broader investigation: d5a05c2b040984afe5294feb84ac8323b14357889bd5982c572d6361aeaafd30. Generic enum payload equality and ordinary boxed-class Reflect.field handling remain separate scopes.","files":null}]}